    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d3">

                <pb facs="0128" n="2" xml:id="pg2"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A4b/311</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bancroft</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-03-06"><hi rendition="#italic">March 6,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received March 24.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Foreign Service Report</seg>
                    <lb/>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 15</seg></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>On February 24th, 1925, the Minister for Foreign Affairs informed the Ambassador,
                    in Note No. 22, a copy of a translation of which is enclosed herewith,<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d3-f1"/> that on February 9th, 1925, the Japanese Government
                    completed the work of scrapping their vessels of war as provided for in the
                    Treaty for the limitation of Naval Armament signed at Washington in February.
                    1922. A list, similar to that issued by the Japanese Navy Department, giving the
                    names of the vessels which were scrapped and the manner in which they were
                    disposed of was enclosed in the Foreign Minister’s Note. A copy of this list is
                    also enclosed herewith.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d3-f2"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
